hey everyone dogan here and this is the  the new office the first video in the  new office it's amazing i've been  getting a lot of questions about the  house the number one question being why  are the ceilings so low in certain  places and i'd like to go to the  transition space between my office my  studio and the rest of the house in  order to answer that now so the reason  that they're so low in some spaces is  because when you go through an area with  the low ceiling it makes the areas  without the the low ceilings with the  high ceilings  feel  way more spacious than they actually are  99 of the time that i'm in here i'm just  walking from this door into my office  you know i've got like a little desk  here that i can maybe do some work at  and uh you know i put some more like my  japanese books up here but i would never  be standing in this area for more than  two or three minutes and when i go  through a space like this that feels a  little bit intentionally cramped  then  when i go into the office  which has this much higher ceiling this  ceiling feels  way higher than it actually is this is  actually a  design kind of cue from traditional  japanese architecture the way that i  understand it is that older japanese  houses i think it might have been the  chests the tea rooms they would have  these very very low uh windows well very  low doors such that samoday samurai  couldn't easily get into the room  while holding their swords you have to  duck through  and get in it's kind of a security  measure and as a byproduct of that what  would happen is when you  when you were crouching down and then  look and once you finally got through  that and into the room it made the room  feel  way bigger than it actually was and so  this again my understanding of it kind  of became a staple of japanese  architecture in well traditional  japanese architecture in certain places  make certain places that you aren't in  for a long time  feel a little bit cramped a little bit  maybe cozy to use a euphemism such that  when you get to the the where you're  going the room that you are in for a  long time it feels way bigger than it  actually is now there is one exception  to this kind of low ceiling you're only  in the place with a low ceiling for a  limited period of time and that's this  room this is the guest room the reason  that the ceilings are so low in here is  actually it's a building regulation so  if the ceilings are any higher the space  gets treated as a room but if they're  under a certain height then the space  gets treated as  a monoki kind of like a kind of like a  storage space or just kind of like a  maybe that kind of like an attic is the  best way to think about it  and in keeping the the ceilings low in  here we were able to stay within the  laws that dictate  how  the the overall total surface area of  a house in japan because this is a guest  room because it's it's meant to be a  place that people are mostly just  sleeping i imagine when you know i'm  filming a video with mr yabadan or you  know like chris or any of my other  youtube buddies that come out and stay  here we're going to be filming you know  down in the big room  we're going to be filming in my office  we're going to be filming it in  different areas out in the courtyard and  then this room again is mostly just for  coming up at night and sleeping so  that's when the ceilings are the way  that they are in certain areas of the  house i know it's definitely not for  everyone but when i saw the same  architectural principles being applied  at the the model house and heard the  explanation i thought it was really cool  and i wanted to try and do something  similar here and i'm really happy with  how it turned out going to be making a  lot more videos about the house in the  near future about the cost of materials  etc if you have any questions feel free  to leave a comment below alright talk to  you guys soon  [Music]  you
 

